From 63479dd542e8f2c549a56747b6b305b01c55cc5b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 10:27:39 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ingress: observe a declared WorkerCoordinator claim against
 the committed registry before the crossing

StegVerse-Labs/.github#3369 gap 2 ("stale head / fence"), option 2C of
design/INGRESS_GAP_OPTIONS_3369.md. #3369 assigns claim/fence to the
Registry/WorkerCoordinator owner; ingress observes that state and mints
nothing.

A manifest may declare `extensions.stegverse_workercoordinator_claim:
{"claim_id": "<id>-G<fence>", "fencing_token": <fence>}`. It lives under
`extensions` because the pinned SDK's validate_ingress_manifest refuses any
other top-level field as unknown before this operation sees the manifest
(tests/test_organization_ingress_worker_claim.py::SdkValidatorTests proves both
halves against the SDK's own example manifest). receive() checks the
declaration before cross() against the committed WorkerCoordinator truth,
control/worker-registry.json (the registry
heartbeat_runtime/coordination_ledger.py validates claim coverage from), with
the shape rule of heartbeat_runtime/process_adapter.py:
- malformed declaration, unreadable or other-schema registry, no task or two
  tasks holding the claim -> FAIL_CLOSED FENCE_DECLARED_NOT_VERIFIED;
- a task holds the claim id but is not BOUND, is archived or terminal, or
  holds another fence -> DENY FENCE_STALE_OR_RELEASED;
- an absent declaration is admitted and the result reports
  worker_claim_state: UNVERIFIED, as the boundary admits and records an
  unattested crossing; a verified one reports
  VERIFIED_AGAINST_COMMITTED_WORKERCOORDINATOR_REGISTRY.
Predicate DECLARED_FENCE_IS_THE_CURRENT_WORKERCOORDINATOR_FENCE; every refusal
carries the six fields and is recorded at both ledger levels through _refused.

Redelivery (I-10): a declared claim that no longer verifies is not refused when
this repository's ledger already records the admission of the exact request;
the replay path answers it as the recorded transition and the result reports
RECORDED_ADMISSION_REPLAYED_FENCE_NOT_RECHECKED. Without that, a fence that
moved after an admission would mint a DENY refusal beside a recorded ALLOW for
the same request.

The result also reports the SDK derivation's own
requires_workercoordinator_claim_fence for the route
(workercoordinator_claim_fence_required_by_route). A required-but-undeclared
claim is still admitted as UNVERIFIED; turning that into a refusal is the route
owner's call and is not made here. Receipt bytes are unchanged: the state is
reported on the result only (asserted), on the plain, role-conformance and
governance returns alike.

tests/test_organization_ingress_worker_claim.py drives the real receive(),
crossing and process_boundary.py against temporary ledger roots and a temporary
checkout holding the registry (25 tests, 13 subtests): malformed and unheld
declarations, stale and released fences, redelivery after the fence moved,
receipt bytes, ordering after the authority-effect check, the pinned SDK
validator, and that every BOUND claim in the committed registry verifies at
its own fence and at no other. n4's spec_gap now records the fence half as
implemented (COVERED_WITH_SPEC_GAP); the remaining gap is the head half,
option 2A (ORGANIZATION-BATCH-CUSTODY-REPLAY-001 readback of the declared
predecessor).

Refs StegVerse-Labs/.github#3369, #3195

